      "## Abandoned carts: the money already on the table",
      "The biggest job of an ecommerce chatbot is also the most valuable: recovering abandoned carts. A customer who added a product to their cart has already done the hard part. They found the store, they chose the product, and they were ready to pay. Then something stopped them: the delivery charge, a question about the size, a payment method they did not trust, or simply a distraction. Most stores do nothing about this. They send one email, if they send anything at all, and the customer never comes back. A chatbot changes the pattern. When a cart is abandoned, it can message the customer on WhatsApp within the hour, while the purchase is still fresh in their mind, and ask the question that matters: was it the delivery charge, the size, or something else. If it was a question, the chatbot answers it, from the product information and the delivery and returns policies you have given it. If it was the delivery charge, it can explain the options, including free delivery above a threshold, which is often enough to bring the customer back. And it can send a simple link that takes the customer straight back to their cart, with the product still in it. The conversation feels like a helpful message from the shop, not a marketing email, because it arrives on WhatsApp, where the customer already talks to people. The result is not magic. It is a share of the carts that would otherwise have been lost, recovered, every week, without your team lifting a finger.",
      "## Order tracking and the questions that eat your day",
      "The second job of an ecommerce chatbot is the one that saves your team the most time: answering the questions that arrive all day, every day, and that all have the same answers. Where is my order. When will it arrive. Can I change the delivery address. How do I return this. What is your refund policy. For a busy store, these messages are a full-time job, and they arrive at night, at weekends, and during holidays, when nobody is there to answer them. A chatbot answers them instantly, because it is connected to your order system and your policies. It looks up the order number, tells the customer where the parcel is, and what happens next. It explains the return process, and starts it, without a member of staff reading the same message for the hundredth time. And when the question is unusual, or the customer is unhappy, it hands the conversation to a human, with the order details and the conversation so far attached, so your team does not have to ask the customer to repeat everything. The point is not to remove humans from customer service. It is to remove the repetitive questions from humans, so the humans are free for the customers who actually need one. A store that answers in seconds, at midnight, feels more reliable than a store that answers the next morning, and reliability is what turns a first order into a second one.",
      "## Product questions that sell",
      "The third job of an ecommerce chatbot is the one closest to selling: answering product questions before the customer buys. A customer looking at a product has questions that a product page rarely answers fully. Will this fit a 32-inch waist. Is this compatible with my phone. What is the difference between these two models. Which one is better for a gift. How long does the battery last. If the answer is not there, the customer leaves, and you never know they were there. A chatbot answers from your product catalogue, which means it knows the sizes, the specifications, the colours, the stock, and the price, and it can compare two products side by side, recommend the right one for the customer's need, and suggest a matching product to go with it. It can also check stock and delivery times, so it never recommends something that is not available. This is the same job a good shop assistant does in a physical shop, and it is the job most online stores leave undone, because nobody is there to do it. A chatbot that answers product questions well does not feel like a machine. It feels like a shop that knows its products, and customers buy from shops that know their products. The difference between a store with a chatbot and a store without one is often the difference between a visitor who leaves and a visitor who buys.",
