      "## What American businesses actually pay",
      "The US market has four clear price bands, and knowing which band you are in makes every quote easier to read. The first band is do-it-yourself builders, at $20 to $100 a month: Wix, Squarespace and similar tools are fine for a simple presence, but you build it yourself, you write the copy yourself, and the result usually looks like every other site built on the same template. The second band is freelancers, at $2,500 to $10,000 for a small business site. A good freelancer can build a clean, fast site, but one person is also the designer, developer, copywriter and support desk, so quality depends entirely on who you find, and support can disappear when they take another project. The third band is small agencies, at $10,000 to $30,000. You get a team, a process, and someone to call when something breaks, and this is where most serious small businesses land. The fourth band is larger agencies and custom builds, at $30,000 to $75,000 and above, for sites with custom design, integrations, customer portals or ecommerce. CINEM sits in a different position: an agency build with agency process and support, at $1,499 for a Basic site, $3,999 for Standard and $8,000 for Premium, because the work is done by a full team with lower overheads than a US agency. The price is lower. The process is not.",
      "## What the price buys",
      "A website quote is only meaningful when you know what is inside it, so ask every provider for the same list. A proper build includes custom design, not a bought template with your logo pasted on it, because a template site looks like your competitors and converts like one. It includes the pages your customers actually need: home, services, about, contact, and the service pages that bring in search traffic. It includes copy structure and on-page SEO: titles, descriptions, headings and internal links written so Google can understand what you sell and where you sell it. It includes contact forms that work and are monitored, so an enquiry never sits unread, and a click-to-call or WhatsApp button, because many customers prefer to message before they fill in a form. It includes speed: a site that loads in under two and a half seconds, which is the threshold Google measures and the point where visitors stop leaving. And it includes a handover: you own the code, the domain, the content and the accounts, in writing. A quote that does not list these things is not cheaper, it is incomplete, and the missing pieces will be charged later, usually at the worst possible moment.",
      "## The hidden costs after launch",
      "The build price is not the whole cost, and the costs after launch surprise more owners than the build itself. A domain name costs roughly $12 to $20 a year for a .com, and hosting for a small business site costs $10 to $50 a month for decent shared or managed hosting, or more for a high-traffic site. Then there is maintenance: security updates, backups, broken links, form monitoring and small content changes. Without it, a site slowly decays: a plugin breaks, a certificate expires, a form stops sending, and nobody notices for weeks. At CINEM, Care retainers are $499, $1,499 or $3,999 per month depending on the level of support, and they cover hosting, security, updates, monitoring and ongoing improvement as one service. Content is the other cost people forget. A website with five pages that never changes will not rank for much, and ranking takes regular new pages: our related post How much does SEO cost per month? explains what that work costs. Finally, there is promotion. A website on its own brings in nobody; it needs search, ads or social media to bring visitors, and our Lead Generation service is $599 per month plus ad spend, while our Social Media service is $499 per month. Budget for the first year honestly, not just the launch, because a website is a sales tool you keep, not a poster you print once.",
      "## Why an agency abroad can charge less",
      "The obvious question is how an agency outside the US can build a website for $1,499 when a US agency charges $15,000 for what looks like the same site. The answer is cost structure, not quality. A US agency pays US salaries, US office rent and US overheads, and those costs go into every quote. An agency with a team in a lower-cost market pays less for the same skills, and passes the difference on. The work itself is the same: the same design tools, the same code, the same hosting, the same Google. What matters is not where the team sits, but whether the process is professional: a written scope, a named contact, a staging link you can review, a launch checklist, and support after launch. Time zones are the one real difference, and they cut both ways: work can continue while your office is closed, but you need clear communication and agreed response times, not a developer who disappears for days. Ask for live sites the agency has built, open them on your own phone, and judge the work, not the postcode. A cheap website from a provider who disappears after launch is the most expensive website you can buy, because you will pay someone else to rebuild it.",
