      "Once the kind of website is clear, the price is decided by a short list of things, and it helps to know which ones are expensive. The number of pages matters, but less than people think: ten good pages beat fifty thin ones, and Google rewards depth, not volume. Custom design costs more than a bought template, but a template site looks like your competitors and converts like one, while a custom design is built around your customers and your offer. Content matters too: a site needs real copy, written for your customers, with the services, prices and answers they actually search for, and writing that copy is skilled work, not an afterthought. Features are the biggest price driver: online ordering, payment integration, booking systems, customer accounts, live chat and WhatsApp integration each add work, and a shop with payments costs more than a brochure site for the same business. Two languages, English and Urdu, add design and testing time, because Urdu text needs proper right-to-left layout and room to breathe. And speed matters: a site that loads slowly on mobile data loses customers before they read a word, and building a fast site takes more care than building a slow one. None of these is a reason not to build a website. The point is to choose the features your customers will actually use in the first version, and to leave the rest for a later update.",
