      "## The enquiries a property chatbot answers",
      "The first job of a real estate chatbot is to answer the same questions instantly, every time, without tying up an agent. A buyer who messages about a listing wants to know the price, the size, the number of bedrooms and bathrooms, the location, whether it is furnished, what the monthly charges are, and whether the price is negotiable. A renter asks about the deposit, the advance, the lease length, and whether pets or families are welcome. An investor asks about the rental yield, the payment plan, and the expected completion date. These are all questions with clear answers, and a chatbot that knows your listings can answer them in seconds, with photos and a location link, while your agent is out on a viewing. The difference between an instant answer and an answer three hours later is often the difference between a viewing and a lost buyer. Speed is not a nice extra in real estate, it is the product, and a chatbot is the cheapest way to buy it.",
      "## Listings, viewings and bookings",
      "Answering questions is only the first half. The second half is turning the conversation into a viewing, and this is where a property chatbot earns its money. A good chatbot does not just answer, it guides. It asks the buyer what they are looking for, shows the listings that match, and when the buyer likes one, it offers the next available viewing times and books one, taking the name, number and preferred time, and sending a confirmation on WhatsApp. It then reminds the buyer before the viewing, so fewer appointments are missed, and after the viewing it follows up to ask how it went and whether they want a second viewing or an offer. All of this happens without an agent touching a keyboard, and the agent's time is spent where it matters: at the viewing, with the buyer, closing the deal. The chatbot should be connected to your listings, so prices and availability are always current. A chatbot that quotes an old price or books a viewing for a property that is already sold does more harm than good, and keeping the listings up to date is the one job the agent must never skip.",
      "## Lead qualification: the part that pays",
      "Not every enquiry is worth an agent's time, and that is the part of the job most agents underestimate. A chatbot can qualify every lead before a person ever sees it, by asking the questions that matter: what is your budget, which areas are you considering, are you buying or renting, when do you need to move, and are you a cash buyer or do you need finance. The answers sort the enquiries into serious buyers, early browsers, and time-wasters, and the agent sees the serious ones first, with the full conversation attached, so they can call the right person with the right information. This matters most for agents who advertise heavily, because ads bring in a lot of enquiries, and answering all of them by hand is impossible. A chatbot turns a flood of enquiries into a short list of qualified buyers, and that short list is what the agent actually works. The qualification questions should be short and natural, not a form in disguise. Three or four well-chosen questions are enough, and the chatbot should let a buyer skip ahead to a person whenever they want, because a buyer who is ready to talk is a buyer you do not want to slow down.",
      "## WhatsApp follow-up: where deals are won and lost",
      "In real estate, the follow-up is where most deals are won and lost, and it is also where most agents fail, because they are busy with the next viewing. A buyer who viewed a property on Monday and heard nothing by Wednesday has already moved on. A chatbot that follows up on WhatsApp changes that. It sends a reminder before the viewing, a thank-you message after it, a nudge when a similar property is listed, and a gentle check-in when a buyer goes quiet. It can also send new listings that match what the buyer asked for, so the buyer hears from you first when the right property appears. The key is that the follow-up is personal and relevant, not a blast message sent to everyone. A chatbot that knows what the buyer asked for, what they viewed, and what their budget is can send messages that feel like they come from an agent who remembers them, and that is what keeps a buyer with you instead of with the next agent. At CINEM, our chatbot setups include WhatsApp follow-up as part of the monthly service, because a chatbot that answers but never follows up is only doing half the job.",
      "## What it costs and what to watch for",
      "A real estate chatbot from CINEM starts from $999 setup plus $299 per month. The setup covers the configuration, your listings and answers, the WhatsApp and website connections, and testing with real enquiries. The monthly cost covers the conversations, the hosting, the updates to your listings and knowledge, and support. When you compare providers, watch for four things. First, does the chatbot actually know your listings, or is it a generic bot with your logo on it. Second, does it book viewings into your calendar and follow up on WhatsApp, or does it only answer questions. Third, who updates it when your listings change, because a chatbot with old listings is worse than no chatbot. Fourth, can you see the conversations, so you know what buyers are asking and where the chatbot needs improving. A provider who will not show you the conversations is a provider to avoid. If you also miss calls from buyers, an AI calling agent from $1,499 setup plus $399 per month can answer those calls and book viewings when your team is busy, and the two systems share the same listings and booking rules, so the buyer gets the same answer whether they call or message.",
      "## Frequently asked questions",
      "How much does an AI chatbot for real estate cost?",
      "A real estate chatbot from CINEM starts from $999 setup plus $299 per month. The setup covers configuration, your listings and answers, WhatsApp and website connections, and testing. The monthly cost covers conversations, hosting, updates to your listings, and support.",
      "Can a chatbot book property viewings?",
      "Yes. A property chatbot can show matching listings, offer the next available viewing times, take the buyer's name and number, book the viewing in your calendar, send a WhatsApp confirmation, and remind the buyer before the viewing. After the viewing it can follow up to ask how it went.",
      "Does it work on WhatsApp or only on my website?",
      "It works on both. Most property buyers message on WhatsApp, so the chatbot should be on your WhatsApp number as well as your website, with the same listings and answers in both places. A buyer who starts on the website and continues on WhatsApp should not have to start over.",
      "Will it replace my agents?",
      "No, and it should not try to. A chatbot handles the repetitive work: answering questions, qualifying leads, booking viewings and following up. Your agents do the work that needs a person: showing the property, understanding the buyer's real situation, negotiating, and closing the deal. The chatbot gives your agents more time with serious buyers, not fewer buyers.",
      "What happens when a buyer asks something the chatbot does not know?",
      "It should say so honestly and hand the conversation to a person, with the full chat attached, so your agent can reply with the context already in front of them. A good chatbot never invents a price, a detail or a promise about a property. The handover rules are set during setup, so your team decides which situations always go to a person.",
      "If your listings are getting enquiries faster than your team can answer them, it is worth an honest look at what you are losing. Tell CINEM at hello@cinem.tech how many listings you handle, where your enquiries come from, and how many viewings you book in a week. We will scope the right chatbot, from a simple listing assistant to a full WhatsApp follow-up system, and price it honestly, starting from $999 setup plus $299 per month."
