      "The price depends on the type of site. A simple clinic website, with services and prices, doctor profiles, opening hours, location and enquiry forms, is a marketing website and starts from $1,499. A clinic website with online appointment requests, patient forms and a simple admin area to manage bookings is web app work and starts from $4,999. A full patient system, with live availability, automated reminders, patient records and follow-up messaging, is custom software, typically $7,999 to $15,000 depending on scope. A chatbot for patient enquiries starts from $999 setup plus $299 per month, and an AI calling agent for missed calls starts from $1,499 setup plus $399 per month. On top of the build, budget for photography, because photos of the clinic and the team are what patients look at first, and for content, because clear service pages and patient information are what bring patients to your site through search. A mobile app starts from $4,999, but most clinics do not need one to begin with, because patients find clinics through Google, not through an app they have never heard of. Our related post Booking website cost: salons, clinics and hotels explains the booking side in more detail.",
      "## Ongoing costs",
      "A clinic website is not a one-off purchase, and it is honest to plan for the running costs. Doctor schedules, services, prices and opening hours change, and someone has to keep them accurate. If the site has online booking, that system needs monitoring, because a broken booking form means lost appointments. Hosting, domain renewal, security and backups are the baseline, and security matters more for a clinic than for most businesses, because patient information is involved. Then there is content: new service pages, health information and patient guides, which are what keep the site ranking on Google. Many clinics handle this with a care plan. At CINEM, Care retainers are $499, $1,499 or $3,999 per month, depending on how much updating, support and ongoing improvement you need. A small clinic with stable information may only need the lower level. A busy multi-doctor practice with online booking and reminders will need more. If you run ads for new patients, our Lead Generation service is $599 per month plus ad spend, and our Social Media service is $499 per month for clinics that want their services promoted properly.",
      "## How a clinic website pays for itself",
      "A clinic website pays for itself in three ways. The first is booked appointments. Every patient who books through your own website is an appointment you earned directly, not one that came through a directory that charges per lead. A clear booking path with instant answers converts far more searches into appointments than a site that makes patients call during office hours only. The second is fewer no-shows and less phone time. Automated reminders cut missed appointments, and clear service and fee information means the reception team spends less time answering the same questions. The third is trust and reputation. Doctor profiles, genuine reviews, clear prices and helpful patient information make your clinic the one patients recommend to family and friends, and that reputation brings new patients month after month. The key is to measure it. Track how many appointments and enquiries come from the website, and how many of those become patients. When you can see those numbers, the website stops being a cost and becomes a clear source of patients, and the decision to invest in a better one becomes easy.",
      "## Frequently asked questions",
      "How much does a clinic website cost?",
      "A professional marketing website for a clinic starts from $1,499 with CINEM. A fuller site with online appointment requests and an admin area starts from $4,999 as a web app. A full patient system with live availability and reminders typically costs $7,999 to $15,000. A chatbot for patient enquiries starts from $999 setup plus $299 per month.",
      "Should a clinic website show consultation fees?",
      "Yes. Patients expect fees to be clear, and hiding them creates fewer bookings, not more. Show the consultation fee, the common treatments and their prices, and say honestly when a price depends on the case. Patients do not expect fees to be low, they expect them to be honest.",
      "Do patients really book online, or do they prefer to call?",
      "Most patients do both. Many prefer to book online, especially outside office hours, and many still call with questions first. A good clinic website offers both: online booking for those who want it, and a clear call or WhatsApp button for those who prefer to talk.",
