      "The job is measurable, which is the point. A landing page has a conversion rate — the share of visitors who do the thing — and that rate is the number that decides whether the page was worth its price. A $500 page that converts at 2% and a $3,000 page that converts at 8% are not in the same league: if the ad spend is $5,000 a month, the expensive page pays for itself in the first month by turning the same traffic into four times as many leads.",
      "## What the price actually buys",
      "Copywriting is the largest part of the value, and the part most buyers underestimate. A landing page is a sales argument in a few hundred words, and the words matter more than the design: the headline, the promise, the proof, and the offer. Good copy comes from research — what the customer is actually worried about, what the competitors promise, and what the business can genuinely deliver. A page built from the client's own description of the business reads like a brochure; a page built from customer research reads like an answer.",
      "Design is the second part, and it is mostly about removing friction: a page that loads fast, reads clearly on a phone, and makes the next step obvious. Most landing page traffic arrives on mobile, from an ad, with the attention span of a thumb-scroll. A beautiful page that loads in four seconds loses to a plain page that loads in one, because half the visitors never see the beautiful one.",
      "Tracking and testing are the third part, and they are what make a landing page a system rather than a poster. Tracking means knowing which ad, which audience, and which page produced each enquiry — without it, you cannot tell whether the page works or the ad works. Testing means trying a different headline, a different offer, or a different form, and measuring which one converts better. A page that is never tested is a guess that was never checked.",
      "## Landing page vs website: which do you need?",
      "If you are running ads, you need a landing page. Sending paid traffic to a homepage is the most common way to waste an advertising budget: the visitor arrives with one question and is handed a menu of twenty answers, and most of them leave. A landing page built for that campaign, with the same promise as the ad and one action to take, is the difference between paying for clicks and paying for customers.",
      "If you are not running ads, a landing page is usually the wrong first purchase. A business with no traffic does not need a better page; it needs a reason for anyone to visit. A website that ranks, a social presence, or a referral programme will do more than a landing page with no visitors. The order matters: audience first, then the page that converts it.",
      "## What makes the price go up — or down",
      "Three things push a landing page price up: custom design rather than a template, copywriting based on research rather than supplied text, and testing and optimisation after launch. A page with a video, an interactive calculator, or a booking flow costs more than a page with a form, because each one is a small piece of software. Integration — connecting the form to a CRM, a WhatsApp flow, or an email sequence — is usually priced separately, and should be: it is a different job from building the page.",
